{{also|Vig|víg}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{clipping|en|vigorish}}, from {{der|en|yi|וויגריש}}, from {{der|en|ru|вы́игрыш||winnings}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/vɪɡ/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɪɡ|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[charge]] taken on [[bet]]s, as by a [[bookie]] or gambling establishment.
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|year=1984|title=Craps|first=John|last=Patrick|isbn=9780930911003|page=11|pageurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=klWntKWFWvQC&lpg=PA11|passage=The house sets '''vigs''' on any game they allow you to bet on.  It is your responsibility to play only those games where the '''vig''' is not prohibitive.  Let's take one more look at how vigorish works for the house.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|year=2009|first=Wayne L.|last=Winston|title=Mathletics: How Gamblers, Managers, and Sports Enthusiasts Use Mathematics|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=QLI3JJ9CD5QC&pg=PA256|page=256|passage=The bookmaker's mean profit per dollar bet is called vigorish or “the vig.” In our example, 11 + 11 = $22 is bet, and the bookmaker wins $1 so the '''vig''' is 1/22 = 4.5%.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|year=2016|first=Mark|last=Andersch|title=By an Addict, for an Addict|isbn=9781524501723|url=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=fFvVDAAAQBAJ&lpg=PT76|passage=The '''vig''' is like your "tax" paid on a bet which goes to the bookie. For example, every $100 I would bet, I had a '''vig''' of $10.  So if I bet $500 and lost that bet, I would owe $550. Trust me, when you don't win, the '''vigs''' add up quick!}}
# {{lb|en|US|_|slang|crime}} Interest from a [[loan shark]]'s loan.
#* '''1973''', [[w:Martin Scorsese|Martin Scorsese]], [[w:Mardik Martin|Mardik Martin]] (screenplay), ''[[w:Mean Streets|Mean Streets]]'', quoted in '''2009''', Ellis Cashmore, ''Martin Scorsese's America'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=mJ-58uKdE7IC&pg=PA118&dq=%22vig%22%7C%22vigs%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=kg8GUqfXJcTKkwWNuoFY&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22vig%22%7C%22vigs%22&f=false page 118],
#*: “You charged a guy from the neighborhood $1800 '''vig'''?” he asks incredulously (“vig” is short for ''vigorish'', meaning a rate of interest from a loan from an illegal moneylender).
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|year=2005|first=F. P.|last=Lione|title=The Crossroads|series=Midtown Blue Book|seriesvolume=2|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=St7_zF_znAcC&pg=PA100|page=100|passage=The guy was probably professional muscle, a leg breaker who collects '''vig''' for a loan shark. (''Vig'' is a mob term for interest on loans to a loan shark.)}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|year=2009|first=Davinia|last=Bostick|title=The Match|isbn=9781441535757|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=2ebsNFIqBpgC&lpg=PA91|page=91|passage="Look, I know I owe you and I know I'm late but I'm good for it. I am. In fact I'll pay you triple what I owe. Triple! Plus the missing '''vigs''', but I need some help first."
}}
# A [[commission]], [[finder's fee]], or similar extra charge.

